B.K9=s(["2020","2025","2030","2050"],t.s)
B.QH=new A.ch("What is Copenhagen's carbon-neutral target year?",B.K9,1,'The passage mentions "Copenhagen aims to become carbon-neutral by 2025."')
B.Kd=s(["A city built in 15 minutes","All services accessible within 15-minute walk/bike","A city with 15-minute commutes by car","A city planning model taking 15 minutes to design"],t.s)
B.QI=new A.ch('What does the "15-minute city" concept mean?',B.Kd,1,'The passage defines it as where "all essential services are accessible within a 15-minute walk or bike ride."')
B.LZ=s(["Ignoring","Struggling to deal with","Celebrating","Measuring"],t.s)
B.QP=new A.ch('The word "grappling" in the first paragraph means:',B.LZ,1,'"Grappling" means struggling or wrestling with a difficult problem.')
B.Ke=s([B.QK,B.QH,B.QI,B.QP],t.hB)
B.R2=new A.tf("Climate Change and Urban Planning",'Cities around the world are grappling with the dual challenge of reducing their carbon footprint while simultaneously adapting to the effects of climate change that are already underway. Urban areas account for approximately 70% of global carbon emissions, making them both a major contributor to the problem and a crucial part of the solution.\n\nProgressive cities have begun implementing ambitious plans to transform their infrastructure. Copenhagen aims to become carbon-neutral by 2025, while Paris has introduced sweeping changes to reduce car dependency, including converting major roads into pedestrian and cycling zones. Singapore is pioneering the concept of a "city in a garden," integrating green spaces into every aspect of urban design.\n\nGreen building standards have evolved significantly in recent years. Modern sustainable architecture goes beyond simple energy efficiency to encompass the entire lifecycle of a building, from the sourcing of materials to eventual demolition. Living walls, rooftop gardens, and passive cooling systems are becoming standard features rather than luxury additions.\n\nPublic transportation remains the backbone of sustainable urban mobility. Cities that have invested heavily in metro systems, bus rapid transit, and cycling infrastructure have seen measurable reductions in per-capita emissions. However, the transition away from private vehicles requires not just infrastructure investment but also a fundamental shift in cultural attitudes toward car ownership.\n\nThe concept of the "15-minute city"\u2014where all essential services are accessible within a 15-minute walk or bike ride\u2014has gained considerable traction. This model not only reduces emissions but also enhances quality of life by fostering stronger community connections and reducing time spent commuting.',B.Ke)
